1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,800
November 2000.

2
00:00:01,800 --> 00:00:03,669
A satellite looks down at a patch of

3
00:00:03,669 --> 00:00:05,050
the Persian Gulf.

4
00:00:05,050 --> 00:00:06,943
There is no island here.

5
00:00:06,943 --> 00:00:07,825
Just sea.

6
00:00:08,208 --> 00:00:10,170
November 2003.

7
00:00:10,170 --> 00:00:11,447
The same place.

8
00:00:11,447 --> 00:00:12,527
A palm tree,

9
00:00:12,527 --> 00:00:13,653
fully formed.

10
00:00:14,095 --> 00:00:15,627
Between these two pictures:

11
00:00:15,627 --> 00:00:16,579
three years.

12
00:00:16,858 --> 00:00:19,551
More than two decades after that first image,

13
00:00:19,551 --> 00:00:23,499
an astronaut aboard the International Space Station photographs

14
00:00:23,499 --> 00:00:24,381
this coast.

15
00:00:24,822 --> 00:00:27,736
The palm-shaped islands stand out from orbit.

16
00:00:28,340 --> 00:00:30,116
So how do you build land where there

17
00:00:30,116 --> 00:00:31,463
was only sea?

18
00:00:31,463 --> 00:00:33,321
And what happened to the plans that came

19
00:00:33,321 --> 00:00:33,948
next?

20
00:00:36,352 --> 00:00:37,873
This is Dubai,

21
00:00:37,873 --> 00:00:39,754
in the United Arab Emirates,

22
00:00:39,754 --> 00:00:41,646
on the shore of the Persian Gulf.

23
00:00:42,157 --> 00:00:44,293
A coastline seems like the one thing on

24
00:00:44,293 --> 00:00:46,395
a map that nobody gets to choose.

25
00:00:46,778 --> 00:00:48,926
Dubai put that idea to the test.

26
00:00:49,309 --> 00:00:50,261
To see how,

27
00:00:50,261 --> 00:00:52,257
we go back to the year 2000.

28
00:00:52,768 --> 00:00:54,103
Here is that year,

29
00:00:54,103 --> 00:00:56,321
seen by an instrument called ASTER,

30
00:00:56,321 --> 00:00:58,352
on NASA's Terra satellite.

31
00:00:58,736 --> 00:01:00,651
The city hugs the shore.

32
00:01:00,651 --> 00:01:03,414
Inland, there is mostly empty desert.

33
00:01:03,925 --> 00:01:06,236
Offshore, where the palm will appear,

34
00:01:06,236 --> 00:01:07,849
nothing has been built at all.

35
00:01:08,232 --> 00:01:11,332
NASA's record includes twelve images of this area

36
00:01:11,332 --> 00:01:14,014
between 2000 and 2011.

37
00:01:14,014 --> 00:01:15,964
This is our baseline.

38
00:01:16,475 --> 00:01:19,598
Dubai's ambitions went far beyond one island.

39
00:01:20,039 --> 00:01:23,267
NASA describes the city building hundreds of artificial

40
00:01:23,267 --> 00:01:24,846
islands along its coast,

41
00:01:24,846 --> 00:01:27,632
to expand its beachfront for tourism.

42
00:01:27,632 --> 00:01:30,616
Plans from the 1990s for one project,

43
00:01:30,616 --> 00:01:32,242
called The World,

44
00:01:32,242 --> 00:01:35,283
included about three hundred small islands.

45
00:01:35,283 --> 00:01:37,570
And as recently as the early 2000s,

46
00:01:37,570 --> 00:01:40,566
NASA notes, little had been built here beyond

47
00:01:40,566 --> 00:01:42,226
shallow Gulf water.

48
00:01:42,226 --> 00:01:43,585
That was the blank page.

49
00:01:43,793 --> 00:01:45,547
February 2002.

50
00:01:46,499 --> 00:01:49,064
About fifteen months after the empty-sea picture,

51
00:01:49,064 --> 00:01:52,304
the first early stage of an archipelago appears.

52
00:01:52,304 --> 00:01:54,080
This is Palm Jumeirah,

53
00:01:54,080 --> 00:01:56,274
the first of Dubai's palm islands to be

54
00:01:56,274 --> 00:01:56,727
built.

55
00:01:57,238 --> 00:01:59,084
By October 2002,

56
00:01:59,084 --> 00:02:00,895
sandy fronds are visible,

57
00:02:00,895 --> 00:02:03,078
with a circular breakwater around them.

58
00:02:03,519 --> 00:02:05,272
That ring is a clue.

59
00:02:05,655 --> 00:02:07,989
This is land with its own defense against

60
00:02:07,989 --> 00:02:08,941
open water.

61
00:02:09,382 --> 00:02:12,493
NASA calls these circular storm barriers.

62
00:02:12,702 --> 00:02:14,339
So where does the land come from?

63
00:02:14,850 --> 00:02:16,556
From the sea floor itself.

64
00:02:17,160 --> 00:02:19,041
Sand was dredged from the bottom of the

65
00:02:19,041 --> 00:02:22,094
Gulf and placed where the island would be,

66
00:02:22,094 --> 00:02:25,589
with rock breakwaters to protect it from erosion.

67
00:02:25,589 --> 00:02:28,259
The material for the island was already there,

68
00:02:28,259 --> 00:02:29,107
underwater.

69
00:02:29,548 --> 00:02:31,870
But loose sand is not ground you can

70
00:02:31,870 --> 00:02:32,624
build on.

71
00:02:33,135 --> 00:02:36,653
NASA's description of the method includes two details.

72
00:02:36,653 --> 00:02:39,927
Ships guided by GPS placed the sand.

73
00:02:39,927 --> 00:02:42,922
And a machine called a vibroflot compacted it

74
00:02:42,922 --> 00:02:45,558
with stone, solidifying the land.

75
00:02:46,162 --> 00:02:50,480
Compare October 2002 with November 2003.

76
00:02:50,480 --> 00:02:53,220
First, sandy fronds inside their ring.

77
00:02:53,603 --> 00:02:55,797
Then, a complete palm.

78
00:02:56,180 --> 00:02:58,085
Just over a year apart.

79
00:02:58,085 --> 00:03:00,093
The pictures can't show us how the work

80
00:03:00,093 --> 00:03:01,533
went day by day,

81
00:03:01,533 --> 00:03:04,170
only where it stood on each date.

82
00:03:04,170 --> 00:03:06,144
But they do show the ring already in

83
00:03:06,144 --> 00:03:08,477
place in October 2002,

84
00:03:08,477 --> 00:03:10,614
while the fronds are still sand.

85
00:03:10,985 --> 00:03:13,284
The buildings and vegetation come later,

86
00:03:13,284 --> 00:03:16,569
in the images from 2004 to 2008.

87
00:03:16,999 --> 00:03:20,261
After 2003, the images change in a different

88
00:03:20,261 --> 00:03:23,779
way. From 2004 through 2008,

89
00:03:23,779 --> 00:03:26,682
ASTER shows buildings and vegetation appearing on the

90
00:03:26,682 --> 00:03:29,340
palm. The land was no longer just a

91
00:03:29,340 --> 00:03:31,616
shape. It was becoming a place.

92
00:03:32,220 --> 00:03:34,553
Palm Jumeirah was only the first.

93
00:03:34,994 --> 00:03:36,910
By September 2006,

94
00:03:36,910 --> 00:03:39,291
NASA was describing the palm islands as the

95
00:03:39,291 --> 00:03:41,636
largest artificial islands in the world,

96
00:03:41,636 --> 00:03:43,366
and every one of them was still under

97
00:03:43,366 --> 00:03:44,167
construction.

98
00:03:44,770 --> 00:03:47,835
That 2006 image shows four projects in a

99
00:03:47,835 --> 00:03:49,740
row, from south to north:

100
00:03:50,123 --> 00:03:51,690
Palm Jebel Ali,

101
00:03:51,690 --> 00:03:53,606
Palm Jumeirah, The World,

102
00:03:53,606 --> 00:03:54,755
and Palm Deira.

103
00:03:55,359 --> 00:03:58,552
Jebel Ali and Jumeirah already look largely complete

104
00:03:58,552 --> 00:03:59,689
as landforms.

105
00:04:00,072 --> 00:04:02,290
The World is still under construction.

106
00:04:02,290 --> 00:04:04,914
Palm Deira is in its earliest stages.

107
00:04:05,239 --> 00:04:08,106
Palm Jumeirah and Palm Jebel Ali were shaped

108
00:04:08,106 --> 00:04:09,604
into palm trees.

109
00:04:09,604 --> 00:04:11,740
The third project is not a palm.

110
00:04:11,740 --> 00:04:13,470
The World is laid out as a map

111
00:04:13,470 --> 00:04:15,084
of Earth's continents.

112
00:04:15,084 --> 00:04:17,545
The plans called for about three hundred small

113
00:04:17,545 --> 00:04:19,681
islands, arranged to draw the planet in the

114
00:04:19,681 --> 00:04:20,192
sea.

115
00:04:20,575 --> 00:04:23,408
Then came 2007 and 2008,

116
00:04:23,408 --> 00:04:24,825
and the global recession.

117
00:04:25,208 --> 00:04:26,218
According to NASA,

118
00:04:26,218 --> 00:04:28,087
it delayed these projects.

119
00:04:28,087 --> 00:04:29,968
In the 2006 image,

120
00:04:29,968 --> 00:04:32,301
you can see how much was still ahead:

121
00:04:32,301 --> 00:04:34,345
Palm Deira had barely begun,

122
00:04:34,345 --> 00:04:36,632
and The World was still under construction.

123
00:04:36,957 --> 00:04:39,534
Now jump ahead to 2022.

124
00:04:39,534 --> 00:04:42,448
NASA's caption for the astronaut's photograph says only

125
00:04:42,448 --> 00:04:44,887
a handful of The World's roughly three hundred

126
00:04:44,887 --> 00:04:46,814
islands had seen development.

127
00:04:46,814 --> 00:04:49,113
That is a statement about 2022,

128
00:04:49,113 --> 00:04:50,947
and we will stay inside it.

129
00:04:50,947 --> 00:04:52,108
We have no reliable,

130
00:04:52,108 --> 00:04:53,954
dated source for what has happened since,

131
00:04:53,954 --> 00:04:55,207
so we will not guess.

132
00:04:56,322 --> 00:04:58,748
Two more projects followed the same pattern.

133
00:04:59,132 --> 00:05:02,545
Palm Deira, in its earliest stages in 2006,

134
00:05:02,545 --> 00:05:05,598
was scaled back and renamed the Deira Islands.

135
00:05:05,598 --> 00:05:06,829
And The Universe,

136
00:05:06,829 --> 00:05:08,315
another planned archipelago,

137
00:05:08,315 --> 00:05:09,673
was put on hold.

138
00:05:09,673 --> 00:05:11,229
Delayed, scaled back,

139
00:05:11,229 --> 00:05:14,294
on hold: those words describe plans,

140
00:05:14,294 --> 00:05:15,908
not the condition of the land.

141
00:05:16,186 --> 00:05:19,902
NASA describes one basic method for Dubai's islands:

142
00:05:19,902 --> 00:05:22,734
dredged sand, protected by rock.

143
00:05:22,734 --> 00:05:24,232
One design is a palm.

144
00:05:24,232 --> 00:05:25,985
The other is a map of Earth.

145
00:05:25,985 --> 00:05:29,155
By 2011, vegetation covered most of Palm Jumeirah's

146
00:05:29,155 --> 00:05:31,871
fronds, and many buildings stood on its trunk.

147
00:05:32,313 --> 00:05:35,169
In 2022, only a handful of The World's

148
00:05:35,169 --> 00:05:36,794
islands had seen development.

149
00:05:37,398 --> 00:05:38,431
Similar method.

150
00:05:38,814 --> 00:05:40,149
Very different outcomes.

151
00:05:40,660 --> 00:05:43,005
What keeps new land from washing away?

152
00:05:43,446 --> 00:05:44,816
In NASA's account,

153
00:05:44,816 --> 00:05:47,649
rock breakwaters protect the islands from erosion.

154
00:05:48,032 --> 00:05:51,167
Around Palm Jumeirah and Palm Jebel Ali,

155
00:05:51,167 --> 00:05:53,524
they form a circular storm barrier:

156
00:05:53,524 --> 00:05:55,172
stone on the outside,

157
00:05:55,172 --> 00:05:56,798
sand on the inside.

158
00:05:57,239 --> 00:05:58,876
Now widen the view.

159
00:05:58,876 --> 00:06:02,475
In 2000, the land inland was mostly empty

160
00:06:02,475 --> 00:06:04,890
desert. By 2011,

161
00:06:04,890 --> 00:06:06,852
NASA reports, roads,

162
00:06:06,852 --> 00:06:10,184
buildings and irrigated land covered almost all of

163
00:06:10,184 --> 00:06:10,997
this area.

164
00:06:11,368 --> 00:06:12,808
The coast was changing,

165
00:06:12,808 --> 00:06:14,631
and so was everything behind it.

166
00:06:15,060 --> 00:06:16,372
A note on method.

167
00:06:16,744 --> 00:06:18,903
Every image here carries its source,

168
00:06:18,903 --> 00:06:21,097
its instrument and its year.

169
00:06:21,608 --> 00:06:23,327
Most come from ASTER,

170
00:06:23,327 --> 00:06:25,080
an instrument on a satellite.

171
00:06:25,521 --> 00:06:27,901
One is a photograph taken by an astronaut

172
00:06:27,901 --> 00:06:29,352
with a Nikon camera.

173
00:06:29,735 --> 00:06:32,731
They are separate pictures from separate days,

174
00:06:32,731 --> 00:06:33,996
not moving footage,

175
00:06:33,996 --> 00:06:36,643
and we never overlay one on another.

176
00:06:37,328 --> 00:06:38,443
For one image,

177
00:06:38,443 --> 00:06:41,450
NASA's text and caption disagree on the month,

178
00:06:41,450 --> 00:06:43,017
so we give only the year.

179
00:06:43,331 --> 00:06:44,933
Lay out the whole record.

180
00:06:44,933 --> 00:06:46,999
Twelve ASTER images,

181
00:06:46,999 --> 00:06:50,354
from November 2000 to 2011.

182
00:06:50,354 --> 00:06:51,376
Empty sea.

183
00:06:51,376 --> 00:06:52,978
The first early stage.

184
00:06:52,978 --> 00:06:55,370
Sandy fronds inside a ring.

185
00:06:55,370 --> 00:06:56,879
A finished palm.

186
00:06:56,879 --> 00:06:59,248
Years of buildings and vegetation.

187
00:06:59,248 --> 00:07:01,198
Each one is a dated fact.

188
00:07:01,198 --> 00:07:03,683
Together, they are a timeline you can check.

189
00:07:04,170 --> 00:07:07,491
NASA says both palm-shaped islands stand out in

190
00:07:07,491 --> 00:07:10,231
the 2022 astronaut photograph.

191
00:07:10,231 --> 00:07:13,284
Both are ringed by circular storm barriers.

192
00:07:13,667 --> 00:07:15,676
But according to NASA's caption,

193
00:07:15,676 --> 00:07:19,193
Palm Jebel Ali remains mostly undeveloped,

194
00:07:19,193 --> 00:07:21,725
though it already looked largely complete as a

195
00:07:21,725 --> 00:07:23,965
landform in 2006.

196
00:07:24,395 --> 00:07:27,309
A finished shape is not a finished city.

197
00:07:27,309 --> 00:07:29,422
So this is not a story of simple

198
00:07:29,422 --> 00:07:31,175
success or failure.

199
00:07:31,175 --> 00:07:33,242
It is a record of what was built,

200
00:07:33,242 --> 00:07:34,530
what was delayed,

201
00:07:34,530 --> 00:07:36,283
and what was only planned.

202
00:07:36,701 --> 00:07:39,371
A map of this coast drawn in 2000

203
00:07:39,371 --> 00:07:41,392
would show open water here.

204
00:07:41,821 --> 00:07:43,342
By November 2003,

205
00:07:43,342 --> 00:07:44,874
that map would be wrong.

206
00:07:45,455 --> 00:07:48,241
The method NASA describes is simple to state:

207
00:07:48,241 --> 00:07:50,238
take sand from the sea floor,

208
00:07:50,238 --> 00:07:51,330
ring it with rock,

209
00:07:51,330 --> 00:07:53,907
and a coastline stops being a fixed line.

210
00:07:54,325 --> 00:07:56,322
It gave Dubai a palm.

211
00:07:56,322 --> 00:07:58,086
It also gave a plan for a map

212
00:07:58,086 --> 00:07:58,713
of Earth.

213
00:07:58,946 --> 00:08:01,442
November 2000: open sea.

214
00:08:02,220 --> 00:08:05,052
November 2003: a palm,

215
00:08:05,052 --> 00:08:06,248
inside its ring.

216
00:08:06,666 --> 00:08:09,894
In 2022, an astronaut photographs the palm islands

217
00:08:09,894 --> 00:08:10,741
from orbit.

218
00:08:10,741 --> 00:08:13,423
And NASA's caption notes that other projects were

219
00:08:13,423 --> 00:08:15,165
delayed, scaled back,

220
00:08:15,165 --> 00:08:16,651
or put on hold.

221
00:08:16,651 --> 00:08:18,566
A coastline is not only where the land

222
00:08:18,566 --> 00:08:21,457
ends. Sometimes, it is a decision.

223
00:08:21,457 --> 00:08:22,258
And from orbit,

224
00:08:22,258 --> 00:08:23,454
you can check the dates.

225
00:08:23,930 --> 00:08:26,682
Imagery: NASA Earth Observatory.

226
00:08:26,682 --> 00:08:28,423
ASTER on the Terra satellite.

227
00:08:28,818 --> 00:08:34,461
Astronaut photograph ISS067-E-3785,

228
00:08:34,461 --> 00:08:37,050
ISS Crew Earth Observations.

